From a42928b89a66e7871ce38d1c8ed8d1217f994b73 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 15 Jul 2026 06:21:54 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 08/22] riscv: allow selecting THREAD_SIZE_ORDER without
 VMAP_STACK

Tiny 32-bit systems (2MiB of RAM, XIP kernel) cannot rely on order-1
allocations: with a zone this small, mobility grouping is disabled and
memory fragments into single pages within seconds of boot, after which
every kthread spawn and every fork() fails to allocate an 8KiB stack
even with hundreds of KiB free:

  Normal: 181*4kB (M) 0*8kB 0*16kB ... = 724kB
  kthreadd invoked oom-killer: gfp_mask=0x400dc0(GFP_KERNEL_ACCOUNT|
  __GFP_ZERO), order=1

VMAP_STACK is the usual answer, but it is 64-bit only on RISC-V: 32-bit
still handles vmalloc mappings by lazily faulting the missing PGD entry
in (do_page_fault -> vmalloc_fault), and a lazy fault cannot be taken on
the stack the trap handler is about to push to.  x86 has the same
limitation for the same reason.

Let EXPERT kernels choose THREAD_SIZE_ORDER=0 without VMAP_STACK
instead.  With IRQ_STACKS enabled (the default), hard and soft IRQs run
on their own per-cpu stacks, so a 4KiB task stack bounds only the
syscall depth, which is the m68k model.  The default for every
configuration is unchanged.

Co-developed-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 arch/riscv/Kconfig | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/arch/riscv/Kconfig b/arch/riscv/Kconfig
index 3378150d3..eb2ea1f80 100644
--- a/arch/riscv/Kconfig
+++ b/arch/riscv/Kconfig
@@ -859,7 +859,7 @@ config IRQ_STACKS
 	  overflows. We may save some memory footprint by disabling IRQ_STACKS.
 
 config THREAD_SIZE_ORDER
-	int "Kernel stack size (in power-of-two numbers of page size)" if VMAP_STACK && EXPERT
+	int "Kernel stack size (in power-of-two numbers of page size)" if EXPERT
 	range 0 4
 	default 1 if 32BIT
 	default 2
